META
id=demo-newtonian-mechanics
title=Newtonian Mechanics
bestUnitTestPercent=-1.0

FLASHCARDS|N=8
FC|demo-fc-1|Newton's First Law|What does Newton's first law say about motion?|An object keeps a constant velocity unless a net external force acts on it. This means rest and uniform straight-line motion are both natural states. For example, a hockey puck on nearly frictionless ice keeps moving until friction or another force changes its velocity.
FC|demo-fc-2|Newton's Second Law|How should F = ma be interpreted?|The vector sum of all external forces equals mass times acceleration. Acceleration points in the direction of the net force. A 2 kg cart with a 6 N net force accelerates at 3 m/s^2.
FC|demo-fc-3|Free-body diagrams|What belongs on a free-body diagram?|Draw only forces acting on the chosen object, with one arrow for each external interaction. Do not include motion arrows or forces the object exerts on something else. Choose axes that simplify components before writing equations.
FC|demo-fc-4|Normal force|Is the normal force always equal to weight?|No. The normal force is the contact force perpendicular to a surface and depends on the situation. On level ground with no vertical acceleration N may equal mg, but on an incline or accelerating elevator it generally does not.
FC|demo-fc-5|Friction|How do static and kinetic friction differ?|Static friction adjusts up to a maximum value to prevent slipping, while kinetic friction acts after surfaces slide. The common models are f_s <= mu_s N and f_k = mu_k N. Always decide whether the surfaces are actually sliding before choosing a model.
FC|demo-fc-6|Newton's Third Law|How can third-law force pairs be identified?|Third-law forces are equal in magnitude, opposite in direction, and act on different objects in the same interaction. Because they act on different objects, they do not cancel on a single object's free-body diagram.
FC|demo-fc-7|Inclined planes|Why resolve weight into components on an incline?|Choosing axes parallel and perpendicular to the incline turns gravity into components mg sin(theta) along the slope and mg cos(theta) perpendicular to it. This often makes the normal-force and acceleration equations much simpler.
FC|demo-fc-8|Circular motion|What provides centripetal acceleration?|Centripetal acceleration is not a new force. It is the inward acceleration produced by the net inward component of real forces such as tension, gravity, friction, or the normal force. Its magnitude is v^2/r.

PRACTICE|N=5
Q4|demo-p-1|Newton's Second Law|0.45|false|MCQ|A 4 kg cart experiences 14 N east and 6 N west. What is its acceleration?|0|2 m/s^2 east||4|2 m/s^2 east|5 m/s^2 east|2 m/s^2 west|20 m/s^2 east|4|Correct. The net force is 8 N east, so a = Fnet/m = 8/4 = 2 m/s^2 east.|This uses the 14 N force without subtracting the opposing 6 N force.|The direction of the net force is east, not west.|Force divided by mass gives acceleration; multiplying gives the wrong units and value.
Q4|demo-p-2|Newton's First Law|0.40|false|MCQ|A probe drifts in deep space with negligible external force. What happens to its velocity?|1|It remains constant||4|It slowly decreases to zero|It remains constant|It steadily increases|It reverses direction|4|No net force means no acceleration, so the velocity remains constant.|Correct. Newton's first law predicts constant velocity when net external force is negligible.|No net force means no acceleration, so the speed does not steadily increase.|Nothing in the situation creates a reversal of direction.
Q4|demo-p-3|Friction|0.55|false|MCQ|A box is pushed horizontally but remains at rest. Which statement about static friction is best?|2|It matches the needed opposing force up to its maximum||4|It must equal mu_s N in every case|It is zero because the box does not move|It matches the needed opposing force up to its maximum|It points in the same direction as the attempted motion|4|Static friction does not automatically take its maximum value.|Static friction can act even when there is no slipping.|Correct. Static friction adjusts to prevent slipping until its maximum is reached.|Static friction opposes the tendency for relative slipping.
Q4|demo-p-4|Newton's Third Law|0.60|true|MCQ|A skater pushes a wall and accelerates backward. Which force is the third-law partner to the skater's push on the wall?|3|The wall's push on the skater||4|The skater's weight|The floor's normal force on the skater|The wall's force on the floor|The wall's push on the skater|4|Weight is part of a different gravitational interaction.|The floor normal force is a different contact interaction.|This is not the reciprocal force pair for the skater-wall interaction.|Correct. The wall pushes the skater with equal magnitude and opposite direction.
Q4|demo-p-5|Inclined planes|0.65|true|MCQ|A frictionless block rests on a 30 degree incline before release. Which component of gravity drives it down the slope?|1|mg sin(30 degrees)||4|mg cos(30 degrees)|mg sin(30 degrees)|mg|zero|4|mg cos(theta) is perpendicular to the incline.|Correct. The component parallel to the incline is mg sin(theta).|The full weight is not parallel to the slope.|There is a nonzero component of gravity along the incline.

UNITTEST|N=8
Q4|demo-u-1|Newton's Second Law|0.55|false|MCQ|A 6 kg sled accelerates at 2.5 m/s^2 east while friction is 3 N west. What applied force acts east?|0|18 N||4|18 N|15 N|12 N|3 N|4|Correct. The net force is 15 N east, so the applied force must be 18 N east to overcome 3 N of friction.|15 N is the net force, not the applied force.|This does not provide the required 15 N net force after friction.|The applied force must be larger than friction when the sled accelerates east.
Q4|demo-u-2|Free-body diagrams|0.50|false|MCQ|Which item should not appear as a force arrow on a free-body diagram of a falling ball?|2|The ball's downward velocity||4|Earth's gravitational force on the ball|Air resistance on the ball|The ball's downward velocity|A contact force if the ball touches a surface|4|Gravity is a force acting on the ball.|Air resistance is an external force when relevant.|Correct. Velocity describes motion, not an interaction force.|A contact force belongs on the diagram when contact exists.
Q4|demo-u-3|Normal force|0.60|false|MCQ|A person stands on a scale in an elevator accelerating upward. Compared with mg, the normal force from the scale is...|3|greater than mg||4|equal to zero|less than mg|equal to mg|greater than mg|4|The scale must exert an upward force while supporting the person.|If N were less than mg, the net force would be downward.|Equal forces would give zero vertical acceleration.|Correct. Upward acceleration requires N - mg > 0, so N > mg.
Q4|demo-u-4|Friction|0.65|true|MCQ|A 10 kg crate slides on level ground with mu_k = 0.20. Using g = 9.8 m/s^2, what is the kinetic-friction magnitude?|1|19.6 N||4|2.0 N|19.6 N|49 N|98 N|4|The coefficient is dimensionless and must multiply the normal force.|Correct. N = mg = 98 N and f_k = 0.20 times 98 N = 19.6 N.|This is not 0.20 times the 98 N normal force.|98 N is the normal force, not the friction force.
Q4|demo-u-5|Newton's Third Law|0.60|false|MCQ|Earth pulls a satellite inward by gravity. What is the third-law partner force?|0|The satellite pulls Earth gravitationally||4|The satellite pulls Earth gravitationally|The satellite's centripetal acceleration|The satellite's velocity|A second inward force on the satellite|4|Correct. The same gravitational interaction gives equal and opposite forces on Earth and the satellite.|Acceleration is not a force and cannot be a third-law partner.|Velocity is not a force.|A third-law pair acts on two different objects.
Q4|demo-u-6|Circular motion|0.75|true|MCQ|A car moves around a flat curve at constant speed. What horizontal force can provide the required inward acceleration?|2|Static friction from the road||4|The car's weight|The road's normal force|Static friction from the road|A separate centripetal force in addition to all real forces|4|Weight is vertical on a flat road.|The normal force is vertical on a flat road.|Correct. Static friction can point inward and provide the net centripetal force.|Centripetal force describes the inward net force; it is not an extra force.
Q4|demo-u-7|Inclined planes|0.70|true|MCQ|A frictionless block slides down a 37 degree incline. Which expression gives its acceleration along the slope?|1|g sin(37 degrees)||4|g cos(37 degrees)|g sin(37 degrees)|g|g tan(37 degrees)|4|The cosine component is perpendicular to the incline.|Correct. F_parallel = mg sin(theta), so a = g sin(theta).|Only the component of gravity parallel to the incline accelerates the block along it.|The tangent is not the gravitational component ratio used here.
Q4|demo-u-8|Net force|0.80|true|MCQ|An object moves right but has a net force to the left. Which statement must be true at that instant?|3|Its acceleration points left||4|It must already be moving left|Its speed must be increasing|Its velocity is zero|Its acceleration points left|4|Velocity direction and acceleration direction can differ.|With acceleration opposite the current velocity, speed may be decreasing rather than increasing.|A nonzero velocity is compatible with a leftward net force.|Correct. Acceleration always points in the direction of net force.

